  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Durable Solutions for the Most Vulnerable Host Populations, Refugees and Migrants in Djibouti</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Djibouti has experienced sustained economic growth for several years. However, this growth has not translated into improved living conditions and the current unemployment rate stands at 39%, with a major incidence among women (66%) and young people (63%). The population is growing at a high rate, fuelled by strong demographic growth (birth rate at 27%) and a high migration flow.The Republic of Djibouti has always been an important transit country for migrants from the Horn of Africa to Yemen and the Gulf countries. Despite the conflict in Yemen, which intensified from March 2015, many migrants, mostly Ethiopians, continue to pass through Djibouti in order to reach the Arabian Peninsula. In addition, some migrants also transit through Djibouti to return home, after an attempt to emigrate, which is often aborted. Djibouti is also a destination country for migrants, mainly Ethiopians and Somalis, who flee poverty, insecurity and persecution. Unfortunately, there are no exact figures on the scale of the phenomenon. This migrant population includes young women who come alone to work as domestic servants in the capital or separated or unaccompanied children. The latter often find themselves living on the streets where they are exposed to many risks. The overall objective of the project is to protect refugees and migrants and provide sustainable solutions for refugees, migrants and host populations in the context of the CRRF and the commitments made by the Government of Djibouti in these areas.</narrative>
  </description>
